// CARD a6-01 · SEE: raised wework lettering on a grey wall above glass doors; the camera pushes toward the name · SEAM in right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@reel;under:wework_sign_miami_brickell;ua:0;move:in;z:.12;in:right"
In early twenty twenty-four, the founder tried to buy WeWork **back**.

// CARD a6-02 · SEE: the real report of the bid (Reuters via The Straits Times, 26 March 2024) lies dim across the top; a bid card is raised on "five": $500M+ · MARCH 2024; on "fifty" a higher gold one: $650M · APRIL 2024 · SEAM out right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@bid_cards;doc:wework_doc_post_neumann_bid_2024_03_26;crop:.03,.4,.97,.6;cards:$500M+^MARCH 2024 · REPORTED^five~$650M^APRIL 2024 · REPORTED^fifty;out:right"
His offer was reported at more than five hundred million dollars, then six hundred and fifty **million**.

// CARD a6-03 · SEE: an index card lands on the desk with the letter's claim typed on it: “partnering with Third Point” — Neumann’s lawyers’ letter, Feb 2024 · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@claim_vs_denial;claim:“…partnering with Third Point…”^Neumann’s lawyers’ letter, February 2024^partnering;in:right"
His letter said he was partnering with Dan Loeb's hedge fund, Third **Point**.

// CARD a6-04 · SEE: the same desk: on "Third" Third Point's answer lands beside it — “only preliminary conversations” and no commitment — and a red line strikes the claim on "commitment" · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@claim_vs_denial;claim:“…partnering with Third Point…”^Neumann’s lawyers’ letter, February 2024^-;reply:“only preliminary conversations” · no commitment^Third Point, to the FT, February 2024^Third;strike:commitment;out:right"
But Third Point said it had made no **commitment** to pay. (J)

// CARD a6-05 · SEE: a man in a checked suit, bag in hand, walks away toward an iron gate and the street · SEAM in right
# cine act=after fx="code:@reel;clip:wework_clip_ipo_leaving_2;move:in;z:.08;in:right"
So the creditors chose their own deal, and in May, Neumann walked **away**.

// CARD a6-06 · SEE: Neumann on a dark stage behind, dim; his statement lights word by word as the quote voice reads it; ADAM NEUMANN · VOICED, via NYT DealBook, 28 May 2024 · SEAM HOLD
# cine act=after fx="code:@post_quote;bg:wework_neumann_2015_disrupt_wide;q:“The company looks to be emerging from bankruptcy with a plan that appears unrealistic and unlikely to succeed.”;go:company;span:5;size:60;by:ADAM NEUMANN · via NYT DealBook, 28 May 2024 · read by a voice actor"
@neumann: The company looks to be emerging from bankruptcy with a plan that appears unrealistic and unlikely to succeed.

// CARD a6-07 · SEE: a long wooden WeWork reception desk with a Welcome to WeWork screen, the camera easing in · SEAM out right
# cine act=after fx="code:@reel;under:wework_interior_toronto_reception;ua:0;move:in;z:.08;out:right"
{grave} In June twenty twenty-four, WeWork came out of bankruptcy, owned mostly by Yardi, a property software **company**.

// CARD a6-08 · SEE: in an empty, lit lobby a small real stack of cash lands on "thirty-seven" ($337M · YARDI, FOR ABOUT 60%); on "ten" a dark wall builds beside it and runs out of frame ($10B+ · SOFTBANK, PUT IN OR PROMISED) · SEAM in right
# cine act=after fx="code:@cash_vs_wall;plate:wework_art_building_lobby;cash:wework_clip_cash_bills_table__100;a:$337M^YARDI · FOR ABOUT 60%^thirty-seven;b:$10B+^SOFTBANK · PUT IN OR PROMISED^ten;note:what control of WeWork cost, before and after;in:right"
Yardi paid three hundred and thirty-seven million dollars for about sixty **percent**.
SoftBank had put in, or promised, more than ten billion before the stock market **listing**.

// CARD a6-09 · SEE: a row of new apartment blocks above the trees, the camera rising past their balconies · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;clip:wework_clip_flow_building_cloudy;move:up;z:.1;out:right"
{excited} Then, in April twenty twenty-five, Flow raised more than one hundred million dollars **more**.
The reported value: about two and a half **billion**.

// CARD a6-10 · SEE: rain on a city street at night; on "desks" a lit window rises out of the dark with people working at a shared table inside; on "owners" we push through it into the room — the opening image, returned · SEAM in right
# cine act=after fx="code:@framed_insert;outside:stock_city_rain_city_02;inside:wework_clip_cowork_discussion;rise:desks;fill:owners"
{warm} So, back to that wet morning in Manhattan. The desks are still there, rented by the month, under new **owners**.

// CARD a6-11 · SEE: black. The thesis arrives word by word in large serif and holds dead still; "month" in gold · SEAM hard cut, HOLD
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@thesis_black;lines:It rented for years~and sold by the month.;go:fell;span:1.8;hi:month"
WeWork didn't fall because of one man's ego. It fell because it rented for years and sold by the **month**.

// CARD a6-12 · SEE: black. The second half of the thesis arrives the same way and holds; "price" in gold · SEAM hard cut, HOLD
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@thesis_black;lines:And the one paying for the growth~set the price.;go:investor;span:2;hi:price"
And because the investor paying for the growth was also the one who set the **price**.

// CARD a6-13 · SEE: a key on a blue tag slides into a front-door lock and turns; it slows on "door" · SEAM in right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@reel;clip:stock_realestate_keys_handover_06;move:in;z:.12;slow:door;in:right"
The founder survived for a simpler reason: a valuation isn't cash, and a famous name opens the next **door**.

// CARD a6-14 · SEE: hands counting cash on a table, dimmed; THE LESSON; question 1 slides in on "who" — WHO SET THE PRICE?; question 2 on "much" — HOW MUCH CASH CHANGED HANDS? · SEAM out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@two_questions;bg:stock_money_raining_07;lab:NEXT TIME YOU HEAR A VALUATION, ASK;qs:WHO SET THE PRICE?^who~HOW MUCH CASH CHANGED HANDS?^much;out:right"
So here's the lesson. When a founder story quotes a valuation, ask who set that price, and how much cash changed **hands**.

// CARD a6-15 · SEE: the glass doors of a new apartment building, a tower blurred above; the camera eases toward the doors · SEAM in right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@reel;clip:wework_clip_flow_building_location;move:in;z:.1;in:right"
Flow says it may go public one day, and if it does, it will have to file its own S-1, with every number, for anyone to **read**. [BEAT]

// CARD a6-16 · SEE: the two accounts a last time: left WeWork's page 41, read in public (WEWORK · READ IN 2019), right Flow's glass doors (FLOW · NOT YET) — the founder's side still blank · SEAM in right, out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@two_accounts;l:wework_doc_s1_lease_47bn_p41;r:wework_clip_flow_elevator;lt:WEWORK · READ IN PUBLIC;rt:THE FOUNDER · FLOW;lv:S-1 · 2019;lat:read;rv:NOT YET FILED;rat:founder's;open:no;in:right;out:right"
{awe} And for the first time, we'll all finally get to read the founder's own account, line by line, in public, the way the world once read **WeWork's**.

// CARD a6-17 · SEE: the Manhattan skyline at dusk, lights coming on; dead still, the camera barely moving · SEAM in right, HOLD to the end card
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@reel;clip:wework_clip_nyc_skyline_dusk;move:none;z:.02;in:right"
Should a founder get a second chance this fast? Tell us in the **comments** below.
